# Emery Plugin - Emery Workflow

## Overview

This plugin provides the core Emery workflow skills: initialize a project, plan a change, execute the plan, check where a plan stands, and finalize. Each skill is an ultrathin wrapper that invokes the matching `emery` CLI verb and relays its output; `/emery:execute` wraps the `emery plan execute` loop (opens `plan.execute.started` and drives refine → build → merge per entry under gap gates), and `/emery:status` is the read-only "where am I / what next" probe over computed progress.

**Purpose**: Drive the `plan → execute → finalize` rhythm. Refine, build, and merge are internal phases of the execute loop, not operator commands.

## Source / target adapter structure

Adapters split into two roles:

- **Source adapters** (`adapters/sources/<name>/`) supply input — `survey` (plan time) and `extract` (slice time). Examples: `intent`, `documentation`, `typescript`, `screenshots`.
- **Target adapters** (`adapters/targets/<name>/`) consume artifacts — `guidance`, `build`, and `merge`. Examples: `omnia`, `vectis`, `contracts`.

Each directory contains operation prompts under `prose/prompts/`; adapter identity and metadata come from the compiled component (`describe`), not a manifest file.

## Slice lifecycle

The execute loop drives each slice through the fixed `refine → build → merge` phases, with `emery plan drop` as the abandon path. Lifecycle labels project from phase timestamps, artifacts, and facts — they are not stored status fields on `metadata.yaml` / `plan.yaml`:

```text
refining -> refined -> built -> merged
    \          \         \
     `--------> `-------> `-------> dropped
```

- **refining**: Slice directory created; the refine phase's extract + synthesis in flight
- **refined**: Proposal, spec, design, and tasks are validated (`base.yaml` pins recorded)
- **built**: Build record + wave facts present; ready to merge
- **merged**: Wave committed, deltas applied, slice archived
- **dropped**: Slice archived without merging into baseline

Per-entry `pending` / `in-progress` / `done` are the same kind of projection (claims + merge/archive facts).

## Workflow

```text
/emery:init <target>               (once per project)

/emery:plan <name> source ...      (authors change.md + plan.yaml; exits for operator review)

/emery:execute                     (the refine -> build -> merge loop; CLI equivalent:
                                   emery plan execute — opens the authorization epoch)

/emery:finalize <name>             (confirm publication, archive plan)
```

At any point, `/emery:status` (read-only, wraps `emery plan status`) reports where the plan stands and the literal next command — use it whenever unsure what to do next.

There are no per-slice breakout commands. When the loop stops (`refine-failed`, `build-failed`, `merge-conflict`, …), fix the reported problem and re-run `emery plan execute` — the loop resumes at the parked phase. A refined slice whose recorded pins drifted is re-refined automatically under the new epoch. Other repair surfaces stay on the CLI:

- `emery plan drop <slice> [--reason "<why>"]` — abandon one entry's slice without merging.
- `emery plan amend <slice> --allow-composition-replace true` — authorize a whole-document composition overwrite for that entry's merge.
- `emery plan defer <slice>/<req> --reason "<why>"` — durably defer a named open gap requirement out of build scope (`--retract` reopens it).
- `emery plan execute --gap-policy defer` — one-epoch policy override: disposition every open gap at the build gate instead of stopping.
- `emery slice list | validate | provenance | model show` — read-only slice projections.

## Tracing and output

Tracing is selected by the reserved log flags — never set `RUST_LOG` on an `emery` subprocess. The CLI strips `--debug` / `--quiet` anywhere in argv before the verb parses it, so the flags are position-independent and never collide with a verb's own options. Two normal modes, selected by verb:

- **Long-running orchestrations** (`emery plan author`, `emery plan execute`) run bare — the host default emits live INFO progress on stderr with the noisy OTel targets muted:

```text
emery plan author <name> ...
```

- **Probes and short deterministic verbs** (`emery --version`, `emery plan status`, `emery slice list`, `emery slice validate`, `emery plan drop`, `emery plan archive`, `emery init`) pass `--quiet` so the result card is uncluttered:

```text
emery plan status --quiet
```

**Debug mode**: when the operator explicitly asks for debug (says "debug", or passes `debug` / `--debug` on the slash command), pass `--debug` on **every** `emery` subprocess in that skill invocation, probes included (it replaces `--quiet` — the two flags are mutually exclusive):

```text
emery plan author <name> ... --debug
```

`--debug` turns on the INFO progress filter plus `omnia_cursor` / `omnia_wasi_http` debug tracing; both flags win over any ambient `RUST_LOG`.

Stream roles: the semantic CLI result (text or JSON envelope) is stdout; command failures and live tracing are stderr. Relay the semantic result or structured error once, verbatim; do not repeat or reformat tracing lines in the closing response.

## Troubleshooting

### Common error codes

Every CLI failure prints a kebab-case error code (and usually a `hint:` line — follow it). The codes met most often:

| Error code | What it means | Next action |
|------------|---------------|-------------|
| `init-adapter-required` | `emery init` was run without naming a target adapter | `emery init <adapter>` |
| `emery-version-too-old` | The installed binary is older than the project's floor | Reinstall via the installer script (the hint prints the literal command), then rerun |
| `adapter-cli-too-old` | An adapter requires a newer binary than the one installed | Reinstall the binary; if the adapter itself is stale, `emery adapter upgrade <name>` |
| `plan-already-exists` | `/emery:plan` on a project that already has a `plan.yaml` | Continue the existing plan (`/emery:status`) or confirm the replace and re-run with `--force` |
| `plan-execute-stopped` | The execute loop halted on a stop condition; the stop card names the reason | Fix the reported problem, then re-run `emery plan execute` — the loop resumes at the parked phase |
| `plan-gaps-unresolved` | Open unknowns or conflicts block the gap gate before build under the `strict` gap policy | Fix inputs and re-run execute, defer named gaps with `emery plan defer <slice>/<req> --reason "<why>"`, or run `emery plan execute --gap-policy defer` |
| `merge-postflight-failed` | The merge landed but the post-merge check failed; the entry is already `done` | Repair the baseline, then `/emery:execute` to acknowledge and continue |

### Project not initialized

- Run `/emery:init <target>` in the project root to create `.emery/` and write `project.yaml`
- Verify `.emery/project.yaml` exists

### Slice not found

- Inspect `.emery/slices/` to see active slices
- Check the slice name matches (kebab-case)

### Artifacts incomplete

- Run `emery slice validate <name>` to check artifact status (pin drift and baseline drift surface here as review advisories)
- Re-run `/emery:execute` — the loop re-refines affected slices under the epoch
- Re-run `/emery:plan <name> ...` to author a fresh change

